<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang={locale(assigns)}>
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title(assigns)}</title>
    <%!--
      What a link to this page says when it is pasted into a club's WhatsApp
      group, which is how most people arrive here. Before this it said
      nothing: the URL, and a browser tab reading "OpenResults".

      Every value comes from `OpenResultsWeb.Meta`, which builds it out of
      the snapshot and honours the arbiter's display switches while doing it
      - a city or a set of dates they turned off must not leak out through a
      preview card, where nobody who could notice would ever see it.

      No image. A share card without one is a small card rather than a broken
      one, and inventing a picture of a tournament this server has never seen
      would be this site making something up.
    --%>
    <meta :if={description(assigns)} name="description" content={description(assigns)} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title(assigns)} />
    <meta :if={description(assigns)} property="og:description" content={description(assigns)} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta :if={canonical_url(assigns)} property="og:url" content={canonical_url(assigns)} />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="OpenResults" />
    <meta property="og:locale" content={OpenResultsWeb.Locale.og_locale(locale(assigns))} />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
    <%!-- A tournament that is reachable but not yet approved for an audience -
          see `OpenResultsWeb.Plugs.Visibility`, which sends the same thing as
          `X-Robots-Tag`. --%>
    <meta :if={assigns[:noindex]} name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <link rel="icon" href={~p"/favicon.svg"} type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href={~p"/assets/css/app.css"} />
    <%!--
      There was no script tag here at all, and the reason was good: these
      pages are read-only, carry no session, and hundreds of phones load them
      over a playing hall's wifi, so a result should be readable the moment
      the HTML lands.

      That promise is intact. What follows is not an application - there is
      no bundle, no socket, no framework and no request on load. It is a
      preference reader that runs before first paint (inline, so there is no
      round trip and no flash of the wrong theme) and, further down, a
      right-click handler and a refresher, both of which only ever act after
      the page is already readable.

      Every page still renders completely with all of it switched off: the
      theme falls back to the device's own light/dark setting, and a player's
      name is still a link to their card. Nothing here is load-bearing, which
      is the condition under which adding it was worth it.
    --%>
    <script>
      (() => {
        const KEY = "openresults:theme";
        const root = document.documentElement;

        // localStorage THROWS rather than returning null where a browser
        // blocks site data - a private window, or a partitioned third-party
        // frame, which is exactly the club-embed case this site supports.
        // Unguarded, that would take the whole bootstrap down.
        const read = () => { try { return localStorage.getItem(KEY); } catch (_) { return null; } };

        const saved = read();

        // A round reached with `?display=1` is the projector view - see the
        // round template - and needs the high-contrast theme regardless of
        // what this visitor (or this browser) has otherwise chosen: the room
        // decides this, not a preference set at a desk somewhere else. Checked
        // first so it outranks both a saved choice and the device's own
        // scheme; the picker below is still there, one tap away, to switch it
        // back for this viewing.
        const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
        const projector = params.get("display") === "1" || params.get("display") === "true";

        // No choice made yet means LIGHT, not "follow the device".
        //
        // Following the device is the better default for an application
        // somebody lives in. This is a page a spectator opens once, often
        // embedded in a club's own site - and a club site is overwhelmingly
        // light, so a dark slab dropped into the middle of one reads as
        // broken rather than as a theme. The same argument applies to a
        // projector in a hall, which is the other place these pages end up.
        //
        // "Match device" is still there, one click away, and once chosen it
        // wins and persists. Only the default changed.
        if (projector) {
          root.setAttribute("data-theme", "contrast");
          // "user" rather than "default": the picker highlights whichever
          // dot matches these two attributes, and "High contrast" is the
          // true answer to "what theme is this" here, chosen or not.
          root.setAttribute("data-theme-source", "user");
        } else if (saved) {
          if (saved === "system") {
            // Deliberately no attribute: the stylesheet's
            // prefers-color-scheme query is what decides, and writing
            // "system" into the attribute would stop it matching.
            root.setAttribute("data-theme-source", "system");
          } else {
            root.setAttribute("data-theme", saved);
            root.setAttribute("data-theme-source", "user");
          }
        } else {
          root.setAttribute("data-theme", "paper");
          root.setAttribute("data-theme-source", "default");
        }

        root.classList.add("has-js");

        // One voice for every script on the page - see #announcer in the body.
        // Cleared first and filled a beat later, so the same sentence twice
        // in a row ("updated just now") is announced twice rather than
        // swallowed as no change.
        window.openResultsAnnounce = (text) => {
          const region = document.getElementById("announcer");
          if (!region || !text) { return; }
          region.textContent = "";
          setTimeout(() => { region.textContent = text; }, 100);
        };
      })();
    </script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <%!-- First on the page, so the first Tab reaches it: past the brand, the
          three languages and the theme picker, straight to the table. Out of
          sight until focused - see `.skip-link`. --%>
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main">{gettext("Skip to content")}</a>
    <%!-- Where the scripts speak to a screen reader: the refresher saying the
          page changed or went offline, a filter saying how many rows it left,
          the entry form's FIDE search saying what it found. Outside
          #live-region, because a live region the refresher replaces is a new
          node every time, and a new node is not announced. Always present and
          never `hidden` for the same reason. --%>
    <div id="announcer" class="visually-hidden" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></div>
    <div class={["page", assigns[:display?] && "projector-mode"]}>
      <%!-- The picker lives up here, not in the footer where it started.
            A popover anchored to the last element on the page can only open
            upwards, and an absolutely positioned panel does not grow the
            document - so at the bottom of a long page part of it was simply
            outside the viewport with no way to scroll to it. Up here it opens
            downwards into a page that already has room, which is also where
            the arbiter's app keeps its own. --%>
      <header class="masthead-bar">
        <a class="brand" href={~p"/"}>
          <.logo /> OpenResults
        </a>
        <div class="masthead-tools">
          <%!-- Three links, not a menu. A reader whose browser already asks
                for Dutch never needs this, so it is here for the minority
                whose browser says one thing and whose eyes want another - and
                for anyone pasting a link to the version somebody else can
                read. Plain anchors, so it works with the scripts switched
                off, which the theme picker beside it cannot claim. --%>
          <nav class="lang-picker" aria-label={gettext("Language")}>
            <a
              :for={{code, name} <- OpenResultsWeb.Locale.locales()}
              href={OpenResultsWeb.Locale.switch_path(@conn, code)}
              hreflang={code}
              lang={code}
              title={name}
              class={["lang-opt", code == locale(assigns) && "is-current"]}
              aria-current={code == locale(assigns) && "true"}
            >
              <span aria-hidden="true">{String.upcase(code)}</span>
              <span class="visually-hidden">{name}</span>
            </a>
          </nav>
          <%!-- The same popover the arbiter's app uses, so the two feel like
                one system. The active option is highlighted purely from CSS,
                keyed off the `data-theme` / `data-theme-source` attributes the
                inline script maintains on <html> - no server state, and it
                reflects the choice instantly. --%>
          <details class="theme-picker">
            <summary
              class="theme-picker-trigger"
              title={gettext("Colour theme")}
              aria-label={gettext("Colour theme")}
            >
              <span class="theme-picker-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span> {gettext("Theme")}
            </summary>
            <div class="theme-picker-panel" role="group" aria-label={gettext("Colour theme")}>
              <button
                :for={
                  {key, label} <- [
                    {"system", gettext("Match device")},
                    {"paper", gettext("Paper")},
                    {"night", gettext("Night")},
                    {"board", gettext("Board")},
                    {"slate", gettext("Slate")},
                    {"contrast", gettext("High contrast")}
                  ]
                }
                type="button"
                class="theme-picker-item"
                data-theme-opt={key}
              >
                <span class={"theme-dot theme-dot-#{key}"} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                {label}
              </button>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </header>
      <%!-- `data-live` marks the region the refresher replaces. Everything
            outside it - the theme, the scroll position, an open card - is
            untouched, which is the whole reason this is not a page reload.
            `<main>` around it rather than on it, so the skip link's target
            and the landmark never go anywhere when the region is swapped;
            `tabindex="-1"` so following the skip link moves focus as well as
            the scroll position. --%>
      <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
        <div id="live-region" data-live>{@inner_content}</div>
      </main>

      <footer class="site-foot">
        <span>{gettext("Published by the arbiter. Results are theirs, not this site's.")}</span>
        <%!-- The two things the refresher can say, carried as attributes
              because the script writes them and a string built in JavaScript
              is a string outside the catalogue. Shown here and said through
              #announcer: this span is `hidden` until there is something to
              show, and a region that is not on the page when its text
              arrives is not read out. --%>
        <span
          id="live-status"
          class="live-status"
          data-updated={gettext("updated just now")}
          data-offline={gettext("not updating - connection lost")}
          hidden
        ></span>
        <span class="spacer"></span>
        <%!-- Only on a tournament page the public can see - the controller
              sets it after finding the tournament, so a 404 never carries
              one. See `OpenResultsWeb.ReportController`. --%>
        <a :if={assigns[:report_path]} class="report-link" href={assigns[:report_path]}>
          {gettext("Report this page")}
        </a>
        <%!-- In the footer of a site with no login, because the person asking
              "did my deploy land" is looking at this from outside. A version
              number could not answer it: this app reported the same 0.1.0 for
              weeks while never having been deployed at all.

              A link to the changelog, the same way OpenPairings' own version
              number is one - the build stamp after the version is what
              answers "did my deploy land" and is unchanged by this; clicking
              the whole thing just answers the next question, "land as what". --%>
        <%!-- Named by the version it shows, then by where it goes. An
              `aria-label` of just "Changelog" hid the version from a screen
              reader and left speech input nothing to match: somebody saying
              "click 0.13.0" was saying the one thing the link is not called. --%>
        <a class="build-id" href={~p"/changelog"} title={OpenResults.Build.long()}>
          {OpenResults.Build.id()}
          <span class="visually-hidden">{gettext("Changelog")}</span>
        </a>
      </footer>
    </div>

    <%!--
      The player card, shown in place on right-click.

      Empty until something asks for it, and filled from the player's own
      page rather than from data embedded here - a 200-player open would
      otherwise ship every card to every visitor to serve the handful who
      open one. Left-click still navigates to that page, which is what
      happens with no JavaScript, on a phone, and whenever the fetch fails.
    --%>
    <%!-- Opened by a right-click, or from the keyboard by the context-menu
          key or Shift+F10 on a focused name - both send the same
          `contextmenu` event. Focus goes into the dialog, stays there while it
          is open, and goes back to the name when it closes; see the script. --%>
    <div id="card-overlay" class="card-overlay" data-loading={gettext("Loading…")} hidden>
      <div
        class="card-dialog"
        role="dialog"
        aria-modal="true"
        aria-label={gettext("Player card")}
        tabindex="-1"
      >
        <button type="button" class="card-close">
          {gettext("Close")}
        </button>
        <div id="card-body">
          <p class="card-loading">{gettext("Loading…")}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <script>
      (() => {
        const KEY = "openresults:theme";
        const root = document.documentElement;

        const write = (v) => { try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, v); } catch (_) {} };
        const read = () => { try { return localStorage.getItem(KEY); } catch (_) { return null; } };

        // ---- theme switch ----
        const picker = document.querySelector(".theme-picker");

        if (picker) {
          const trigger = picker.querySelector("summary");

          // The same answer the stylesheet highlights, said to a screen
          // reader as well: which option is the current one. Worked out from
          // the two attributes on <html> exactly as the CSS does it, so the
          // two cannot disagree.
          const markCurrent = () => {
            const source = root.getAttribute("data-theme-source");
            const current =
              source === "system" ? "system" : source === "default" ? "paper" : root.getAttribute("data-theme");

            picker.querySelectorAll("[data-theme-opt]").forEach((option) => {
              option.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(option.dataset.themeOpt === current));
            });
          };

          markCurrent();

          picker.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
            const button = e.target.closest("[data-theme-opt]");
            if (!button) { return; }

            const value = button.dataset.themeOpt;
            write(value);

            if (value === "system") {
              root.removeAttribute("data-theme");
              root.setAttribute("data-theme-source", "system");
            } else {
              root.setAttribute("data-theme", value);
              root.setAttribute("data-theme-source", "user");
            }

            markCurrent();

            // A popover that stays open after you have chosen is a popover
            // you have to dismiss twice. Focus goes back to the trigger: the
            // option that had it has just been hidden, and a focused element
            // that disappears drops the keyboard at the top of the page.
            picker.removeAttribute("open");
            if (trigger) { trigger.focus(); }
          });

          // <details> does not close on Escape by itself.
          picker.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
            if (e.key === "Escape" && picker.open) {
              picker.removeAttribute("open");
              if (trigger) { trigger.focus(); }
            }
          });
        }

        // ---- a form sent back refused ----
        //
        // The page reloads with the reason at the top, and a reader who
        // pressed "Send" from the bottom of the form would otherwise have to
        // find it. Focus goes to the sentence saying nothing was sent; the
        // next Tab is the first field.
        const refused = document.querySelector("#registration-form .alarm, #report-form .alarm");
        if (refused) { refused.focus(); }

        // ---- finding an element again after the refresher replaced it ----
        //
        // The refresher swaps the region's HTML, which replaces every node in
        // it, so "the button that had focus" no longer exists afterwards - only
        // one just like it does. These two find it: `locate` records how to
        // recognise an element (its own identifying attributes, or those of the
        // nearest ancestor that has some, plus the path down from there) and
        // `relocate` looks for the same thing in the fresh DOM.
        const live = document.getElementById("live-region");
        const IDENTIFYING = [
          "id", "href", "name", "data-sort-key", "data-filter", "data-index-search",
          "data-standings-reset", "data-detail",
        ];

        const selectorFor = (el) => {
          const parts = IDENTIFYING.filter((name) => el.hasAttribute(name)).map(
            (name) => `[${name}="${CSS.escape(el.getAttribute(name))}"]`
          );
          return parts.length ? el.tagName.toLowerCase() + parts.join("") : null;
        };

        const locate = (el) => {
          const path = [];
          let node = el;

          while (live && node && node !== live && live.contains(node)) {
            const selector = selectorFor(node);
            if (selector) {
              const index = Array.from(live.querySelectorAll(selector)).indexOf(node);
              return {selector, index, path};
            }
            path.unshift(Array.prototype.indexOf.call(node.parentElement.children, node));
            node = node.parentElement;
          }

          return null;
        };

        const relocate = (spot) => {
          if (!live || !spot) { return null; }
          const matches = live.querySelectorAll(spot.selector);
          let node = matches[spot.index] || matches[0] || null;
          for (const step of spot.path) { node = node ? node.children[step] : null; }
          return node;
        };

        // ---- the player card ----
        const overlay = document.getElementById("card-overlay");
        const body = document.getElementById("card-body");
        if (!overlay || !body) { return; }

        const dialog = overlay.querySelector(".card-dialog");

        // The name the card was opened from, and how to find it again if the
        // refresher replaced it while the card was open.
        let opener = null;

        const close = () => {
          if (overlay.hidden) { return; }
          overlay.hidden = true;
          const loading = document.createElement("p");
          loading.className = "card-loading";
          // From the server, in the reader's language, and set as TEXT - the
          // one place this script writes a whole element rather than moving
          // one the server already sent.
          loading.textContent = overlay.dataset.loading || "";
          body.replaceChildren(loading);
          body.removeAttribute("aria-busy");

          // Back to where the reader was. Without this, closing the card
          // left the keyboard at the top of the page, and a screen reader
          // user who had walked to row 140 had to walk there again.
          const back = opener && (opener.el.isConnected ? opener.el : relocate(opener.spot));
          opener = null;
          if (back) { back.focus(); }
        };

        // Everything in the dialog a Tab can land on, in order.
        const tabbable = () =>
          Array.from(
            dialog.querySelectorAll("a[href], button:not([disabled]), summary, input, select, textarea")
          ).filter((el) => !el.closest("[hidden]"));

        overlay.addEventListener("click", (e) => { if (e.target === overlay) { close(); } });
        overlay.querySelector(".card-close").addEventListener("click", close);
        document.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
          if (e.key === "Escape" && !overlay.hidden) {
            e.preventDefault();
            close();
          }
        });

        // A modal keeps Tab inside it: past the last link back to the close
        // button, and Shift+Tab the other way round. Otherwise the keyboard
        // walks out into a page `aria-modal` has just told a screen reader
        // is not there.
        overlay.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
          if (e.key !== "Tab") { return; }
          const items = tabbable();
          if (!items.length) { e.preventDefault(); return; }

          const first = items[0];
          const last = items[items.length - 1];
          const at = document.activeElement;

          if (e.shiftKey && (at === first || !dialog.contains(at) || at === dialog)) {
            e.preventDefault();
            last.focus();
          } else if (!e.shiftKey && (at === last || !dialog.contains(at))) {
            e.preventDefault();
            first.focus();
          }
        });

        // ---- keeping the page current ----
        //
        // The pages this replaced were LiveViews on a PubSub topic: standings
        // moved as results were entered. This site is static by design, and a
        // spectator refreshing their phone every two minutes was the most
        // visible thing lost in the move.
        //
        // A re-fetch and a swap, not a reload. `location.reload()` would be
        // four lines and would throw away scroll position every 20 seconds,
        // which on a 200-player standings table means losing your place while
        // reading it - worse than the problem.
        //
        // Deliberately does nothing at all on the entry form or the report
        // form: replacing the DOM under somebody who is typing would clear
        // what they had written.
        const status = document.getElementById("live-status");
        const announce = window.openResultsAnnounce || (() => {});
        const isForm = !!document.querySelector("#registration-form, #report-form");
        // The projector view runs its own refresh, on its own slower clock,
        // that swaps only its board rows - see the round template. This one
        // replaces the whole region, which would wipe out which page is
        // showing and the running cycle timer along with it.
        const isProjector = !!document.querySelector("[data-projector]");

        if (live && !isForm && !isProjector) {
          let failures = 0;

          // Somebody in the middle of using a control - a filter dropdown
          // open, a search half typed. Swapping now would close the dropdown
          // under their finger, so this poll is skipped and the next one
          // tries again.
          const inUse = () => {
            const at = document.activeElement;
            return !!at && live.contains(at) && at.matches("input, select, textarea");
          };

          // The page's own wide tables, in order - not the small ones inside a
          // tie-break's <details>, which move with their row when it is sorted.
          const scrollers = () =>
            Array.from(live.querySelectorAll(".scroller")).filter((el) => !el.closest("details"));

          // What a reader has done to the region that its fresh HTML knows
          // nothing about: where focus is, which <details> they opened, how
          // far a wide table is scrolled sideways, what is typed in a box.
          const capture = () => {
            const at = document.activeElement;

            return {
              focus: at && at !== live && live.contains(at) ? locate(at) : null,
              open: Array.from(live.querySelectorAll("details[open]")).map(locate).filter(Boolean),
              scroll: scrollers().map((el) => el.scrollLeft),
              typed: Array.from(live.querySelectorAll("input[type=search], input[type=text]"))
                .map((el) => [locate(el), el.value])
                .filter(([spot, value]) => spot && value),
            };
          };

          const swap = (html) => {
            const doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, "text/html");
            const fresh = doc.getElementById("live-region");
            if (!fresh) { throw new Error("no live region"); }

            // Compare before touching the DOM. Most polls find nothing new,
            // and replacing identical markup would collapse an open <details>
            // and drop focus for no reason.
            if (fresh.innerHTML === live.innerHTML) { return false; }

            const kept = capture();

            live.innerHTML = fresh.innerHTML;

            // Before the scripts below re-run, so they read what the reader
            // had typed rather than an empty box.
            kept.open.forEach((spot) => {
              const details = relocate(spot);
              if (details) { details.open = true; }
            });
            kept.typed.forEach(([spot, value]) => {
              const box = relocate(spot);
              if (box) { box.value = value; }
            });

            // A script embedded in that HTML does NOT run - innerHTML never
            // executes a <script> it inserts - so anything inside
            // #live-region that wires itself up on load (the standings
            // table's sort and filter, currently) would otherwise work once
            // and then go quiet after the first real update. This is the
            // hook: it fires on the region that is always still `live`
            // itself, so code registered against `document` from the page's
            // FIRST render - which does run - can re-query the fresh DOM and
            // wire it up again.
            document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("openresults:updated"));

            // After the scripts, which may have re-sorted the rows the
            // scroll position and the focused element are measured against.
            scrollers().forEach((el, i) => {
              if (kept.scroll[i]) { el.scrollLeft = kept.scroll[i]; }
            });

            // Focus back on the same control, without scrolling to it: the
            // page has not moved, so neither should the reader. Without this
            // every real update dropped a keyboard user's focus to the top
            // of the document - on a sort button, a player's name, anything.
            const refocus = relocate(kept.focus);
            if (refocus) { refocus.focus({preventScroll: true}); }

            return true;
          };

          const tick = () => {
            // Nothing to update while the tab is in the background, and a
            // phone in a pocket should not be polling a server all round.
            if (document.hidden || inUse()) { return; }

            fetch(window.location.href, {headers: {"accept": "text/html"}})
              .then((r) => r.ok ? r.text() : Promise.reject(r.status))
              .then((html) => {
                failures = 0;
                if (status) { status.hidden = true; }
                if (inUse()) { return; }
                if (swap(html)) {
                  // Said quietly and only when something actually changed, so
                  // a spectator knows the page is live rather than stuck.
                  if (status) {
                    status.textContent = status.dataset.updated || "";
                    status.hidden = false;
                    announce(status.dataset.updated);
                  }
                }
              })
              .catch(() => {
                // A hall's wifi drops. Say so rather than showing a page that
                // has silently stopped being current, and keep trying - this
                // is not an error worth ending the session over.
                failures += 1;
                if (failures >= 2 && status) {
                  status.textContent = status.dataset.offline || "";
                  status.hidden = false;
                  // Announced once, when it starts, not on every failed poll
                  // after that.
                  if (failures === 2) { announce(status.dataset.offline); }
                }
              });
          };

          setInterval(tick, 20000);
          // A tab brought back to the front is stale by however long it was
          // hidden, and its reader is looking at it right now.
          document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", () => {
            if (!document.hidden) { tick(); }
          });
        }

        // ---- finding yourself on the FIDE list ----
        //
        // Fills the form's fields; never required, never blocking. Everything
        // it would fill can be typed, and a player who is not on the FIDE list
        // ignores it entirely. The panel is only rendered where this
        // deployment can actually reach an arbiter's list, so there is no
        // "search unavailable" state to explain.
        const fide = document.getElementById("fide-search");

        if (fide) {
          const box = document.getElementById("fide-query");
          const list = document.getElementById("fide-results");
          const none = document.getElementById("fide-none");
          const endpoint = fide.dataset.endpoint;
          let timer = null;
          let latest = 0;

          const field = (name) => document.getElementById("registration_" + name);

          const fill = (player) => {
            // Only what came back. A blank from the FIDE list means "not
            // recorded", and writing it over something the person already
            // typed would be this page deciding it knows better.
            const set = (name, value) => {
              const el = field(name);
              if (el && value !== null && value !== undefined && value !== "") {
                el.value = value;
              }
            };

            set("name", player.name);
            set("rating", player.rating);
            set("federation", player.federation);
            set("fide_id", player.fide_id);
            set("title", player.title);
            set("birth_year", player.birth_year);

            list.hidden = true;
            none.hidden = true;
            box.value = "";
            // Straight to the one field the list cannot supply, which is the
            // one the arbiter actually needs - and said, because a screen
            // reader otherwise hears "Email, required" with no idea that four
            // fields around it have just changed.
            announce(fide.dataset.filled);
            field("email")?.focus();
          };

          const render = (players) => {
            list.replaceChildren();

            if (!players.length) {
              list.hidden = true;
              none.hidden = false;
              announce(none.textContent.trim());
              return;
            }

            none.hidden = true;
            announce(fide.dataset.found);

            for (const player of players) {
              const li = document.createElement("li");
              const button = document.createElement("button");
              button.type = "button";

              const who = document.createElement("span");
              who.className = "who";
              who.textContent = [player.title, player.name].filter(Boolean).join(" ");

              const about = document.createElement("span");
              about.className = "about";
              about.textContent = [player.federation, player.rating, player.birth_year]
                .filter(Boolean)
                .join(" \u00b7 ");

              button.append(who, about);
              button.addEventListener("click", () => fill(player));
              li.append(button);
              list.append(li);
            }

            list.hidden = false;
          };

          const run = (query) => {
            // Every response carries the number of the request that asked for
            // it. Without that, a slow search for "van" can land after a fast
            // one for "van der berg" and replace the right answers with
            // stale ones.
            const mine = ++latest;

            fetch(endpoint + "?q=" + encodeURIComponent(query), {
              headers: {"accept": "application/json"},
            })
              .then((r) => r.ok ? r.json() : Promise.reject(r.status))
              .then((data) => {
                if (mine !== latest) { return; }
                render(data.players || []);
              })
              .catch(() => {
                // The form works without this. An error about somebody else's
                // server is not something the person typing their name can do
                // anything with.
                if (mine !== latest) { return; }
                list.hidden = true;
                none.hidden = false;
                announce(none.textContent.trim());
              });
          };

          box.addEventListener("input", () => {
            clearTimeout(timer);
            const query = box.value.trim();

            if (query.length < 2) {
              list.hidden = true;
              none.hidden = true;
              return;
            }

            // A request per keystroke would be a search per letter of a
            // surname, against a machine that is also running a tournament.
            timer = setTimeout(() => run(query), 250);
          });

          // The panel is inside the form, so Enter in it would submit a
          // half-filled entry.
          box.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
            if (e.key === "Enter") { e.preventDefault(); }
          });

          document.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
            if (!fide.contains(e.target)) { list.hidden = true; }
          });
        }

        // A right-click on a name - or, from the keyboard, the context-menu key
        // or Shift+F10 on a focused one, which send this same event with the
        // focused link as its target.
        document.addEventListener("contextmenu", (e) => {
          const link = e.target.closest("a.player[data-player]");
          if (!link) { return; }
          e.preventDefault();

          opener = {el: link, spot: locate(link)};
          overlay.hidden = false;
          body.setAttribute("aria-busy", "true");
          // Into the dialog straight away, so a screen reader says "Player
          // card, dialog" while it loads rather than nothing at all.
          dialog.focus();

          fetch(link.href, {headers: {"accept": "text/html"}})
            .then((r) => r.ok ? r.text() : Promise.reject(r.status))
            .then((html) => {
              const doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, "text/html");
              // The SUMMARY section, not the page's first one. The page
              // carries a second, much longer section - the chart, the
              // round-by-round table, every tie-break contribution - and
              // lifting that into a dialog would make the peek and the page
              // the same thing again, which is what this stopped being.
              const section = doc.querySelector("#player-summary");
              if (!section) { throw new Error("no card"); }
              body.replaceChildren(section);
              body.removeAttribute("aria-busy");

              // Then onto whose card it is, if the reader has not already
              // moved on inside the dialog.
              const heading = section.querySelector("h2");
              if (heading && document.activeElement === dialog) {
                heading.tabIndex = -1;
                heading.focus();
              }
            })
            .catch(() => {
              // The card is a convenience; the page behind it is the real
              // thing. Falling through to it beats an error message about a
              // feature the visitor did not know existed.
              close();
              window.location.href = link.href;
            });
        });
      })();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
